{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 6600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar eclipses don't just happen here on \nearth - moons of other planets also pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10520,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between those planets and the sun, resulting \nin various types of solar eclipses on Mars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14400,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, \nand even non-planets like Pluto,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17400,
    "dDurationMs": 1641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eris and various asteroids. So \"where are the \nbest eclipses in the solar system?\" is a question  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19041,
    "dDurationMs": 1639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is best answered with a tier list.\nSo, where are the best eclipses in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar system? For that, we need a tier \nlist from most to least impressive. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24440,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Total solar eclipses on earth are, after \nall, pretty darn impressive: the midday sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28000,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is blotted out and it looks like there's a dark \ngaping hole in the sky - we get to see the sun's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31720,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glowing corona around the inky black dark side of \nthe moon, stars come out, the horizon looks like a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35520,
    "dDurationMs": 10640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sunrise is happening in any direction you look. \nPart of why eclipses are so great on earth is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the sizes and distances of the moon and sun \nwork out so they appear similarly big in the sky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50480,
    "dDurationMs": 2331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the moon can block the sun but not the corona. \nAnd totality happens so rarely on any particular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52811,
    "dDurationMs": 100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place on earth and is over so quickly it's a very \nrare experience and you don't really have time to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52911,
    "dDurationMs": 1809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "process what's going on/what you're experiencing.\nI think this all makes eclipses here on earth a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty superb – aka S tier – phenomenon. So are \neclipses on the other planets in the solar system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any good? Are any of them better than ours?\nLet's start with a non-planet - the Moon. From  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63760,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon, a lunar eclipse (which is when \nthe earth blocks the sun's light from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66880,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon) from the moon, a lunar eclipse is a solar \neclipse. But there's no atmosphere on the moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you don't get any darkening of the sky or \nsunrise in all directions since the sky is dark to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "begin with. And the earth is so big it would block \nthe sun's corona, and take several hours for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77480,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipse to pass. The eclipse would probably feel \nsimilar to lunar night, except that you would see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81120,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dusky red glow around the entire rim of the \nearth due to sunlight scattering through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth's atmosphere. Which is pretty neat. B tier.\nMercury and Venus are next. No moons means no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89520,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses, unless you count the fact that \nMercury sometimes passes between the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Venus, but it's way too small to do \nanything we'd remotely consider to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95480,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipse-like. N tier for \"no eclipses.\"\nMars does have eclipses, but both of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99920,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moons are too small relative to the sun to cause \na total eclipse; Phobos is close enough to Mars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103800,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it can cover as much as 30% of the sun during \neclipses, and you get a lumpy \"ring\" - you'd need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to wear solar glasses to see it, though. C tier. \nThe other moon Deimos is too far away to cover  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112560,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than about 1% of the sun, and appears small \nenough you'd just barely even be able to see it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 20/20 vision through solar glasses. D tier.\nJupiter has some spectacular eclipses - at least,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viewed from space - it has enough moons, and \nbig moons close in, and the moons orbit close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125880,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the same plane as the sun, so that Jupiter \nregularly has eclipses - sometimes multiple at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129680,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same time. But do they look any good from \nJupiter itself... say, floating in its upper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere? Three of the big moons are so big they \nblock the sun and probably most of the corona,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137320,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their shadows would be large enough in \nthe atmosphere you probably wouldn't see much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140160,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"360° sunrise\" either - you would see a lot more \nstars, though - it would look more like nighttime.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143520,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "B tier. Callisto, though, only appears about \none and a half times the size of the sun and so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147880,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would allow you to see the corona and the 360° \nsunrise . Jupiter is also big enough you might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see the surface of Callisto itself illuminated in \nreflected Jupiter-glow - does that make it cooler  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than how our moon looks like a dark hole in the \nsky? I don't know. And the sun and Callisto are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160720,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 5 times smaller viewed from Jupiter than \nour moon viewed from earth... but unlike earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164680,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where eclipses happen only a few times per year, \nCallisto's eclipses happen approximately every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sixteen days for three years straight (and then \nhave a three year break). All in all, I'm giving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172480,
    "dDurationMs": 3753,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Callisto's eclipses on Jupiter A tier. Jupiter's \nsmaller inner moons [Metis, Adrastea, Amalthea,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176233,
    "dDurationMs": 3287,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thebe] all have partial eclipses substantial \nenough to be interesting viewing by the human  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eye with eclipse glasses, so they get C tier, \nwhile all of its outer moons are too small and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far away for their really boring partial eclipses \nto be visible without a serious telescope. F tier. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn has almost 150 known moons, plus rings, \nso it has all sorts of eclipses. About 18 of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193800,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its moons are some combination of big enough \nand close enough to see them from Saturn with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the naked eye - 6 of them are too big and their \neclipses probably look mostly like night time, so,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201560,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "B tier [Tethys Titan Dione Rhea Mimas Enceladus]. \nAnd 8 of them are too small to fully block the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204160,
    "dDurationMs": 3755,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and would require solar glasses; I'm putting the \n\"largest\" 3 of these in C tier [Iapetus, Atlas,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207915,
    "dDurationMs": 1605,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pan] and the others in D tier [Hyperion, \nHelene, Telesto, Daphnis, Calypso]. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209520,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, 4 of Saturns moons are similar enough \nto the sun in their apparent sizes that their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses would be similar to total eclipses on \nearth - you'd see the corona, the deeply colored  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky and 360° sunrise, a dark \"hole in the sky\" \nwhere the sun was (or the moon illuminated by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222360,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn's glow), and so on. I'm putting two of \nthese in A tier (rather than S) because the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226280,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just so much smaller viewed from Saturn [Janus, \nEpimetheus]. But two of them - the moons Pandora  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229600,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Prometheus - are just the right size to create \nboth total and annular eclipses; they're the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moons in the rest of the solar system we know for \nsure can do that other than our moon. Plus they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238600,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have potato-like shapes, so partial and annular \neclipses would be more visually interesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than our boringly spherical moon. S tier.\nEditor Henry here - I just realized that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247320,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what I said about Pandora and Prometheus being the \nonly other moons that can have total and annular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses - that's wrong. Janus and Epimetheus \ncan also both have total and annular eclipses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255520,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and probably Perdita on Uranus, too! Here's what \nI missed: if an eclipse happens at sunrise or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sunset, or near the poles, then the moon causing \nit will be farther away (and thus appear smaller)  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than if it causes an eclipse at noon (and on \nthe equator). And when a moon appears smaller,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it might cause an annular rather than total \neclipse, or reveal more of the sun's corona.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272440,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This \"sunrise shrinking effect\" is technically \ntrue for all moons and all eclipses (and is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277280,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cause of \"hybrid\" annular/total eclipses here \non Earth), but if a moon is far away relative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281320,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the size of the planet then the effect \nisn't particularly big (which is the case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284560,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here on earth). I updated my eclipse calculation \nspreadsheet which now shows Janus and Epimetheus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288840,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can have partial and total eclipses, too! So \nthey are back to S tier. The other place where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292560,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sunrise effect makes a big difference is \non Mars, whose moons are noticeably bigger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overhead and smaller when viewed on the horizon.\nThe rest of Saturn's moons are too small and far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301280,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away to see from Saturn's \"surface\" - \naka upper atmosphere - with the human  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eye - F tier. But Saturn has rings, too! Is \nit technically an eclipse when Saturn's rings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cast a shadow on the planet? I don't know and \nI don't care, it looks spectacular. S tier. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you get past Saturn, you're far enough away \nfrom the sun that it starts to appear more like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star that it is - just a point in the sky \nrather than having a distinguishable disk. I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319240,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even from Saturn the sun only appears 10% as big \nacross as from here on earth. Uranus is almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323680,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twice as far away from the sun as Saturn, so the \nsun appears half as big again, and it's even worse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327400,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Neptune, where the sun only appears slightly \nlarger than Jupiter and Venus do from Earth! "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331120,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the sun so small in the sky, it's a lot \neasier for the apparent size of moons to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "WAAY bigger than the sun - for example, Uranus has \naround 15 moons that appear way too big (relative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the sun) and would result in nighttime-like \ntotal eclipses, with maybe just a little 360°  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343800,
    "dDurationMs": 2566,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sunrise on the best of them; I'm putting them in C \ntier [Ariel Umbriel Miranda Titania Portia Oberon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346366,
    "dDurationMs": 1074,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Puck Juliet Cressida Belinda Desdemona Cordelia \nRosalind Ophelia Bianca]. The moon Perdita has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347440,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the best total eclipse on Uranus (and probably \nalso annular eclipses), and it would be A tier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349840,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except for how small the sun is, so B tier. \nThere are two more moons [Cupid, Mab] that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352920,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren't big enough for total eclipses but whose \nannular eclipses are technically big enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see with solar glasses - D tier. All the \nother known moons are way too small - F tier. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neptune isn't much better - seven of its moons \nappear WAAY too big and completely block the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364680,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun - D tier [Triton Despina Galatea Proteus \nLarissa Thalassa Naiad]. Hippocamp is actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a great size, appearing just barely bigger \nthan the sun and probably has 360° sunrise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370640,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "views of the corona, and the rest - again, B \ntier because of how small the sun is. The rest of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374520,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neptune's known moons are too small to see: F tier \n[Nereid Halimede Sao Laomedeia Neso Psamathe]. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377600,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, we get to Pluto - yeah, it's not a \nplanet, but it still has eclipses. Problem is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381480,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they suck/they're horrible. Pluto has five known \nmoons, and the smallest two [Styx \u0026 Kerberos] are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually the perfect size for just-barely-total or \nmostly-full-annular eclipses - except from Pluto,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388920,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sun appears as just a point of light in the \nsky (it's essentially a really bright star),  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392680,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you couldn't see any detail of partial \nor annular eclipses without a telescope.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395960,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there's no atmosphere, which means no \n360° sunrise or the like for total eclipses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399840,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the sun is probably too small for the corona \nto look like more than a star either. Basically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403240,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if Styx or Kerberos fully eclipse the sun, it \njust feels like normal nighttime. And when Styx  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407680,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or Kerberos have annular eclipses, you wouldn't \nnotice anything other than a slight dimming of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun. D tier. The other three moons [Charon, Nix, \nHydra] appear substantially bigger than the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and easily eclipse it - especially Pluto's double \ndwarf planet partner, aka it's oversized moon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charon. Given the minuscule size of the sun \nviewed from Pluto and Pluto's lack of atmosphere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423800,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these are not at all interesting eclipses. The \nonly reason they're not F tier is that they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427120,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technically an eclipse you can see. So, D tier.\nAnd we're not done yet! There are still other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430960,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses! Eris (another dwarf planet even farther \nout than Pluto) has one known moon, but it's so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much bigger than the sun and Eris also has no \natmosphere that the eclipse is definitely D tier. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439400,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are also a lot of double asteroids in our \nsolar system and there's enough variety in their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sizes and distances and such that some of the \n\"moonlets\" appear much bigger than the sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some much smaller, and some very close in size. \nThey're mostly closer to the sun than Jupiter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450400,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some get as close as Earth, so the sun is a \ndecent size in their skies; but they all suffer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454280,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a similar problem as Pluto and Eris - \nno atmosphere, which makes for fairly boring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458240,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses. The best of these are probably C tier.\nIf you want to get really nerdy, of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462360,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's possible for moons of planets like Jupiter \nand Saturn to eclipse each other, too... but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466400,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evaluating the relative distances and apparent \nsizes relative to the sun for all possible moon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combinations and positions for hundreds of moons \nis definitely beyond the scope of this video. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473920,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that completes the tier \nlist. Thanks for watching!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
